    {
      "id": 9,
      "category": "INTRODUCTION",
      "categoryName": "Chillus ൻ (n) / ൺ (ṇ)",
      "title": "Chillus: ൻ and ൺ",
      "type": "introduction",
      "completed": false,
      "section": 5,
      "content": {
        "steps": [
          {
            "heading": "What are chillus?",
            "characters": [
              { "malayalam": "ൻ", "iso15919": "n", "description": "Chillu N: dental nasal with no vowel. Used at the end of words.", "isNew": true },
              { "malayalam": "ൺ", "iso15919": "ṇ", "description": "Chillu Ṇ: retroflex nasal with no vowel. Used at the end of words.", "isNew": true }
            ],
            "embeddedTask": {
              "label": "Quick check",
              "prompt": "Which chillu comes from the retroflex nasal ണ?",
              "options": ["ൻ  (n)", "ൺ  (ṇ)"],
              "correct": 1
            }
          },
          {
            "heading": "Match syllables to their chillus",
            "matchingExercise": [
              { "left": "ന",  "right": "ൻ",  "desc": "Dental nasal" },
              { "left": "ണ",  "right": "ൺ",  "desc": "Retroflex nasal" }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "insight": "A chillu is a consonant with its inherent vowel completely removed. ന has the vowel 'a' built in; ൻ does not. You learned about chandrakkala (്) in Module 2, which also removes the vowel: ന് (n + chandrakkala) and ൻ (n-chillu) are identical sounds. The difference is where they're used: chandrakkala forms appear in consonant clusters (middle of words), while chillus appear only at the end of words. Both represent a consonant with no inherent vowel."
      }
    },
